+\subsection{Conditional effective region and loss of coverage}
+\label{sec:effective}
+
+The positive result concerns a declared neighborhood, not an informal
+description of attack strength. For a transformation $a$, let $P_a$ be the
+units actually supplied to the frozen detector after transformation.
+If these units are a legal partition of the same token tape with
+$P_a\in\mathcal A_k(x,P_0)$, the rendering and scoring contract is preserved,
+and a completed exact evaluation gives $C_k(x)>\tau$, then
+$D(x,P_a)>\tau$. The guarantee follows from the verified minimum; it does
+not require that the boundary movement be visually small.
+
+For a fixed threshold and evaluated radii $0,\ldots,K$, a detectable document
+with $C_0(x)>\tau$ has the conditional certified radius
+\begin{equation}
+k^\star(x;\tau)=
+\max\{k\in\{0,\ldots,K\}:C_k(x)>\tau\}.
+\end{equation}
+Nesting of the threat sets makes all radii up to $k^\star$ valid.
+This is a definition of an effective region, not an observed positive
+radius on the final real cohorts. If no evaluated radius passes, the set is
+empty; if scoring was not completed, the radius is unestimated.
+Separately recalibrated $\tau_k$ do not imply a monotone empirical TPR curve.
+
+A still narrower invariance fact holds when an intervention delivers the
+same ordered, byte-identical units to the same deterministic detector: its
+score and decision are identical. Coarse boundary preservation is weaker.
+Even mild lexical paraphrase can change an embedding, AMR, or adjacent
+relation, so it needs its own evidence or additional stability assumptions.
+
+Deep paraphrase, paragraph reordering, sentence insertion/deletion, and
+cross-sentence fusion can change tokens, order, or unit count. When they do,
+they violate the present contract and its guarantee cannot be invoked.
+This is a loss of certificate coverage, not a theorem that every
+sentence-dependent watermark must miss the rewritten text. A method may
+retain signal or recover it through different units or reconstruction;
+that possibility requires evaluation under a separately declared contract.
+
