 \section{Discussion}
 
+\subsection{Effective region versus empirical failure boundary}
+
+The audit distinguishes three statements. First, the saved SWAN short-text
+configuration has an observed decision failure under five-to-one merging,
+explained sufficiently by its trial-count ceiling. Second,
+Section~\ref{sec:effective} gives a conditional positive guarantee for all
+legal partitions whose completed minimum passes the fixed threshold.
+Third, transformations outside that set have no guarantee from this
+certificate; their detection performance remains an empirical question.
+
+These statements do not establish that a particular segmentation policy
+maintains TPR and controls FPR whenever coarse sentence structure survives.
+That would require a frozen comparison across policies and transformations,
+with independent quality and null evaluation. Sentence, paragraph,
+sub-sentence, and event units are candidate policies for such a study, not
+policies ranked by the current experiments. Likewise, no general
+deep-rewriting failure curve or universal impossibility result is available.
+
+The resulting research focus is to identify which interface conditions
+support a stated guarantee and which perturbations violate them.
+Segmentation is an intermediate assumption of decoding and evaluation;
+its inclusion in a contract is not a barrier against arbitrary strong
+rewriting.
+
